{
 "id": "2026-11-24-a",
 "title": "Scientists hacked human eyes to see infrared 👁️",
 "description": "Your eyes only catch light from about 400 to 700 nanometers, so near-infrared light is invisible to you. In 2019, researchers (University of Science and Technology of China and UMass Medical School, in the journal Cell) injected tiny upconversion nanoparticles into mice's eyes: the particles turn ~980 nm infrared into green light, and the mice could see infrared for up to 10 weeks. In 2025, Tian Xue's team (USTC, Cell) put the same kind of nanoparticles into contact lenses, and people could see flashing near-infrared light. They saw it even better with their eyes closed, because near-infrared passes through the eyelid better than visible light. The catch: it only works with bright infrared sources like LEDs, and fine detail is blurry. Research, not a product, and not medical advice.\n\nSources: Cell Press / ScienceDaily (2025); Physics World (2025); ScienceDaily (2019); Physics World (2019).",
 "tags": [
